00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.380
about Two Sigma in this now.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:59.090
So Two Sigma is a financial
firm and a tech firm both.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:03.770
Broadly, our mission
is to get huge amounts

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.300
of useful, indicative
privacy-preserving financial

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.360
data, to draw
inferences from that,

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.720
and to optimize
economic outcomes.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.940
We're certainly known
as a hedge fund,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.690
but we do a lot more than that.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.390
For example, we
are using data now

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.050
in a very rapidly growing
insurance operation, where

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.520
we're looking to greatly
almost revolutionize

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:32.300
the way that small
business insurance is done

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.770
to assist in capital formation
and reduce overheads,

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:36.830
et cetera.

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:40.400
Any case, we have very broad
and interesting challenges

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.990
in our space.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.440
We collect almost a
petabyte of data a month.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.870
So it's a fairly
large operation.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.510
And to do this, we
feel that we need

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.830
to be a tech company as
well as a finance company.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.980
And that's a complicated
balance to achieve.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:02.360
We can't be at the
back end of technology,

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.420
as some finance companies
have traditionally been.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.460
Otherwise, you won't
have the talent

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.710
to be at the leading
edge of our industry.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:14.420
But if we adopt every new
tech thing without concerns,

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.750
we have $55 billion in
assets under management,

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.970
lots of regulatory
structures, and the like.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.290
So we have to actually
be quite careful as well.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.990
We're about 1,500
people, roughly speaking;

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.240
half engineering in the firm.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.790
So it is really a
fascinating question.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.820
In the security realm, I
think the hardest realm

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.390
of all in the space of
IT or computer science

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:43.010
is how to create an environment
that's incredibly flexible

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.320
and that has appropriate
levels of security.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.200
One of the things we're
most concerned about--

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.660
not the only thing, but one of
them-- is the concern about,

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:58.130
how do we grow as a firm
without the risk of data loss

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.190
growing proportionally with
the number of employees

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:02.570
that we have?

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.250
And we want to do
that while still

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:08.780
having a lot of interaction
among our employees internally,

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.660
so without having to wall
them off from each other

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.810
and prevent sharing, without
having to wall them off

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:17.240
from new technology
or the internet,

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.430
to allow for this
fluid environment

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.600
where we can actually have sort
of our cake and eat it too.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.250
And that's the really
interesting problem.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.210
I suspect that's a problem
for many of you in this room.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:31.070
And we're going to
be talking about part

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.650
of that in this
presentation today

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:37.250
as we talk about how to do
cloud computing in a way that

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:42.080
doesn't have a significant
risk of exfiltration.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.870
One more comment to make--

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.450
we're really
interested in, how do

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:51.626
we make our whole ecosystem
more secure and vibrant?

00:04:51.626 --> 00:04:53.000
That's why we're
giving the talk.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.470
That's beneficial
to us, an I think

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.750
it's beneficial to
others in our industry

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:00.510
and, broadly, to cloud
computing and to Google as well.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.870
So we're doing that
in the hope that we

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.640
can share best practices.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.500
We're giving, I
think, four more talks

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.200
on security over
the next two days.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.730
You can find them--
in fact, we'll

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.610
put up a slide at the
end on other talks

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.130
that we are either giving
or participating in.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.190
We're also giving a
talk on serverless

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:25.030
distributed computing
in the cloud tomorrow.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:28.600
And also, Jaques is
involved in giving a talk

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.320
on Halite, which is a
fascinating AI programming

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:33.370
competition.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.740
We think it will be the
largest-term AI programming

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.860
competition yet, run in the
third incarnation this year.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:41.980
And it explores very
interesting problems

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:46.240
not just about how to get
artificial intelligence

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.990
bots to do as well as people,
but to actually do things

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.580
that people could
never do before.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.830
So self-driving cars,
they're interesting.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:55.300
But we people do self--

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:56.899
we do cars pretty well.

00:05:56.899 --> 00:05:58.690
We have to look at
problems that are harder

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:01.030
than what people can do,
certainly in the investment

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:02.170
management realm.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:04.150
And Halite begins
to get at, what

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:05.630
can we start doing
with computers

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.770
that we can't even conceive
of doing as humans?

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:12.190
All right, without further
ado, let me turn this over now

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.940
to Nick, who will begin
the more specific parts

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:17.180
of the presentation.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:18.763
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS:
Thank you, Alfred.

00:06:20.649 --> 00:06:22.690
Alfred touched a little
on some of our challenges

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.000
and our threat model,
so I'm just briefly

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.280
going to recap some of
the key points there.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:28.930
Those things really
drive the way

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:31.810
we think about security
and security controls.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.820
I'm going to hand
it to Jaques to talk

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.370
about our evolution
and our journey

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:38.717
along the path in heading
into Google Cloud.

00:06:38.717 --> 00:06:41.050
And then, I'm going to spend
quite a bit of time talking

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:43.360
about how we work
tightly with Google

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.210
to build native
security controls

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.670
and implement those controls,
and then finally wrap up

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:49.810
with a few lessons learned.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.450
So a point that Alfred
underscored and I just

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.920
want to quickly reiterate
is that our attack surface

00:06:57.920 --> 00:06:59.710
is very different to
what you'd imagine

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:02.960
for most organizations using
cloud computing resources.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.230
We don't have anything
consumer-facing.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.300
We don't have to be up
and exposed all the time

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:09.049
and worry about DDoS
and things like that.

00:07:09.049 --> 00:07:12.790
Really what we're focused
on is internal use cases,

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:16.270
use of managed services,
processing data.

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:19.120
So we have a very sort of
inside out attack surface

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:22.090
compared to most organizations.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:24.130
Alfred mentioned how key
intellectual property

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:25.120
is for us.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:27.430
And really, when we think
about threat models and risk

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:29.350
mitigation, this
is the factor that

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:34.090
drives a lot of what we
put into as considerations.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:37.730
And when we design controls,
we want to protect our IP.

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:41.110
Again, that's difficult
to do without hampering

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:42.400
people's workflows.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.886
We don't want to take away
access to data or systems

00:07:45.886 --> 00:07:47.260
or segment people
from each other

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.080
so that they can't collaborate.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.302
So mitigating insider
risk is really difficult,

00:07:51.302 --> 00:07:53.260
and one of the things
that's worth highlighting

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.650
is people talk
about insider risk.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.500
And the immediate stretch is
we don't trust our people.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.060
And that's not the case.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:05.440
We definitely want a model
where one single person is not

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.870
in a position to completely
be able to exfiltrate

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:12.010
data or code, but that
also happens accidentally.

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:14.410
And if you've followed
the thread of how

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.974
sophisticated attackers
target organizations,

00:08:16.974 --> 00:08:19.390
really what they're doing is
they're targeting individuals

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.310
with legitimate
access and then using

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:22.870
that compromised
individual's access

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.000
to move laterally,
access their data,

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.639
and ultimately
exfiltrate that data.

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:28.930
So when we think about insider
risk, we think about all

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:29.689
of those cases.

00:08:29.689 --> 00:08:31.730
We're not just thinking
about malicious insiders.

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:34.870
And that's an important point.

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:36.909
Finally, we're not
beholden to many

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:39.580
of the regulatory considerations
that a lot of organizations

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:41.340
working in the cloud are.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:43.270
We don't process
payment card data.

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:46.750
We don't necessarily have
PII or HIPAA considerations.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:50.200
But we are very
strongly consistent

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:55.060
about what our compliance team
is interested in and mandating

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:56.680
that certain aspects
of our business

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:00.160
don't necessarily have access to
data that other businesses are

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:01.390
working on.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:05.020
And this can even go a little
further in our research teams.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:08.140
We're very cognizant
of researcher IP.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.510
And in some cases, you
may have researchers

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:13.550
in the same team working
on the same data set

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:16.549
but not able to view the output
of each other's model code.

00:09:16.549 --> 00:09:19.090
That's really important to us,
so we think about that as well

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:22.130
when we're designing controls.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:24.430
So Jaques will talk a
little about our journey

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:26.620
into the cloud and some of
the challenges we faced.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:32.512
JAQUES CLAPAUCH: So like
many of you, we had a dream.

00:09:32.512 --> 00:09:34.720
And our dream was to connect
directly with the Cloud,

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:38.470
to have a truly elastic internal
infrastructure that extended

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:40.330
to an external infrastructure.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.530
And for that, we, like many
of you, looked to Google.

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.280
Of course, unfortunately,
when we started,

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.220
Google was in its
humble beginnings,

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:50.830
and IAM wasn't
exactly everything

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.670
that we wanted it to be.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:54.610
It was very primitive
and so much so

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:57.610
that they only gave us three
primitive IAM options--

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.590
owner, editor, and viewer.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:01.630
And unfortunately,
that disallowed

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.135
us to go more broadly toward
the scope that we wanted.

00:10:04.135 --> 00:10:06.010
And we wanted things
that were more granular.

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:08.179
There were things in each
of these that we desired

00:10:08.179 --> 00:10:09.595
and things in them
that we didn't.

00:10:09.595 --> 00:10:11.660
And we wanted
combinations of them.

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.680
So at that point,
because of that desire,

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.197
we were unable to
jump into that space.

00:10:17.197 --> 00:10:19.030
And we decided to wait
a little bit until it

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:21.640
was a little bit more mature.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.950
And when it started
getting more mature,

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:26.320
we finally reached
the point that we

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:28.940
felt was enough for us
to actually jump directly

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.820
into Google and be
kind of happy with what

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:33.940
we had with some changes
that we'd have to do.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:36.950
And Google, as it
stood at that point,

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.580
actually had very,
very good services.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:41.859
And it did everything
very, very well.

00:10:41.859 --> 00:10:43.400
But it was, again,
in its beginnings.

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:46.577
And Google does this iterative
model really well in that--

00:10:46.577 --> 00:10:48.160
you can think of it
in terms of a car,

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:49.442
if you're building a car.

00:10:49.442 --> 00:10:50.650
You build really good wheels.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.570
And then, you build a
really good steering wheel.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:54.790
And then, you build the seats,
and then you build the chassis.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:56.140
You don't just make a bad car.

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:57.890
You make very, very
good little parts,

00:10:57.890 --> 00:10:59.380
and you put them together.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:01.850
And we wanted in at
the beginning of that,

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:06.040
so we had to deal sometimes with
driving a car with just wheels.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.800
So when we tried to make
our first connection,

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.600
we saw all these
amazing services

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.910
that, unfortunately, were
all directly connected

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:14.050
to the internet.

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:16.774
And we could use
them, and then we'd

00:11:16.774 --> 00:11:18.190
have an open
gateway, which is not

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:22.310
what we desired if we're trying
to block exfiltration risks.

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:24.700
So we either opened up,
or we didn't use anything,

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:27.110
and we just had VMs everywhere,
which we could really

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:27.860
do without Google.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:30.640
And that's not what we wanted.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.380
On top of that, we didn't
really have a direct connection

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:34.936
to Google at that time.

00:11:34.936 --> 00:11:36.310
Interconnect didn't
really exist.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:37.810
We were in the
type of connection

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:39.760
that just went into
the Google fabric

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:42.730
but not into what we
wanted, into the projects.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:47.110
And that inherently
meant that we

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:49.930
had to do something about that,
in which our case was more

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:52.690
to try to get a VPN
connection in there.

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:55.660
And that required a lot
of tuning from both sides.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:57.790
And we didn't really
need the level of speeds

00:11:57.790 --> 00:11:59.860
that most people need
when they're doing VPNs.

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:01.150
We needed much greater speeds.

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:02.290
Most people, when
they think about it,

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.570
are talking about megabits,
megabytes per second.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:06.130
We are talking
about many gigabytes

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:08.320
per second connections.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:10.060
And of course on
top of all of that,

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.370
Google had these nice
bonuses that were actually

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:13.730
really cool and really useful.

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.360
The first time, for
example, we saw Cloud SSH,

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:17.770
we were in love
with it, the idea

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:19.990
that we could just directly
connect to something.

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:22.674
And we didn't really care
that much at that point

00:12:22.674 --> 00:12:25.090
of that specific exfiltration
risk, because at that point,

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:26.680
exfiltrations are
the speed of typing.

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:28.330
And you can do that in an email.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:29.620
So that wasn't a problem.

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:31.872
Of course, problematically,
we found out

00:12:31.872 --> 00:12:34.330
that there were extra features
that, again, a boon to many,

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:38.440
to us weren't that useful, such
as the Upload File Directly

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:41.230
button in the Cloud SSH.

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:43.900
Other things, such as
serial port connectivity--

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:45.850
serial port is an
amazing feature

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:47.080
if you have network problems.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:48.520
You connect directly to Google.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:52.147
You forego all of
your firewall rules.

00:12:52.147 --> 00:12:53.980
You forego everything,
and you just connect.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:55.813
It doesn't matter if
you have an external IP

00:12:55.813 --> 00:12:58.640
or not, which, of course,
we didn't really want.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:01.630
And it was either go
or no-go for that.

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:03.880
Service account is
another amazing feature

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:07.090
from Google-- also brought
about quite a number of risks.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.280
They come with
refresh tokens that

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.100
didn't expire and could
be reached from anywhere

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:13.232
without any IP restriction.

00:13:13.232 --> 00:13:15.190
And they were short
strings that you could just

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:19.250
write on a piece of
paper and take out.

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:22.300
And on top of that, there
was no egress firewall.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:25.240
You could just send data
outside as much as you wanted.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:28.540
And there were also a number of
overloaded calls at the time,

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:29.790
which, again, it's great.

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:30.630
It's Google magic.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.910
It means that in very few
steps, you can do a lot.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.370
But at the same time
for us, it means that we

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:37.570
lose some of our protection.

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:38.986
For instance, one
great example is

00:13:38.986 --> 00:13:43.150
we had the IAM connection
directly through BigQ Select,

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:43.650
which--

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:45.730
BigQuery Select,
sorry-- BigQuery Select,

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:47.620
which also allows
you to export data.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:50.710
So you either didn't
select things in BigQuery,

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:52.780
or you exported data.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.560
It wasn't a clear
selection within them.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.940
So how could we target all
of these things at once?

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:00.530
And for that, originally,
we proxied everything.

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:02.906
So everything we had to go
through these two proxies, one

00:14:02.906 --> 00:14:05.530
which we called the provisioning
agent and the other one, which

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:06.400
we called Takoyaki.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.310
The provisioning agent was
basically a strong precondition

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:11.290
checker with ordering logic.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:14.350
So it was a direct
REST command that kind

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:16.390
of mapped to some of the
Google ones for things

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:20.338
that we trusted and we knew
wouldn't change over time.

00:14:20.338 --> 00:14:22.880
And it held a very,
very privileged account

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.010
to do all those things
and to change projects.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:27.350
And it only did things the
right way in the right order

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:29.750
such that in no step anything
could be ever compromised

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:31.350
if anything went wrong.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.410
And if anything went
wrong, it did everything

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:35.021
backwards in the right order.

00:14:35.021 --> 00:14:36.770
And it made sure that
no one called things

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:38.645
that they couldn't, such
as creating an image

00:14:38.645 --> 00:14:41.360
with a serial port open in it.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:43.920
Conversely, Takoyaki,
our other tool,

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:47.260
which is kind of just a big
joke, a big pun that we made.

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:49.010
Because originally,
it evolved from squid,

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:51.890
and Takoyaki is really squid
well-done, because it's fried.

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:57.460
It checked REST calls
for specific commands

00:14:57.460 --> 00:14:59.120
and allowed us to
really be more--

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:01.222
allowed us to integrate
directly with gcloud,

00:15:01.222 --> 00:15:02.930
because we could just
allow some commands

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:04.450
and deny other commands.

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:07.654
And it also injected the
tokens into the call so that we

00:15:07.654 --> 00:15:10.070
couldn't--we didn't need to
worry about people just taking

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:13.160
them out, because they
didn't have access to them.

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:15.020
And of course,
both of those meant

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:17.090
that anytime
anything changed, we

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.310
needed to change
everything in our end.

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:21.230
Of course, Google was
awesome and upstreamed

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:23.240
a lot of the things
that we need to change.

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:25.850
But for a lot of
time, that was great.

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:28.970
On top of that,
just Google magic--

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:31.670
you asked for something, Google
goes proof, an it's solved.

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:35.810
So for things like the serial
port access, we talked to them,

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:37.700
and they made
changes that allowed

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:39.320
us to really just turn it off.

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:41.000
Or one other specific example--

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.640
we had what we call
the image exfiltration

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.020
hack at one specific point,
from one day to the other.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:49.070
Again, Google went poof, and we
had this amazing feature that

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:51.740
actually allowed you to just
move images from one project

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:54.260
to another, which is
actually very useful

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:57.170
but, again, not
that much for us.

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:59.210
Because suddenly
that meant that we

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:01.427
could have a very locked
down project with images

00:16:01.427 --> 00:16:03.260
with very, very strong
IP that it could just

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:04.843
copy anywhere and
then just exfiltrate

00:16:04.843 --> 00:16:06.307
if that other project was open.

00:16:06.307 --> 00:16:08.390
Again, Google went poof
again, and we had a button

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:10.670
that allowed us to turn it off.

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:12.170
And on top of
that, we had to add

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:13.700
a little bit of
our special touch,

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.730
so things like SAML, that
allowed us to connect directly

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:19.430
to our IDFS, which meant
that we could log in only

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:22.910
from our internal system there.

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:27.410
So really, it was protected
from external people.

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:31.580
Our OS was custom, so we removed
a lot of the Google SSH stuff

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:34.250
that could also
provide some risks.

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:36.560
For things like G Suite,
we added proxy headers

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:38.870
that only allowed us to go
to our own G Suite things

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:41.000
and disallowed exfiltration.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:45.770
And we had our own proxy black
magic for everything else.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:48.410
Of course, all of those
brought about some hardship

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:52.220
and struggle in the sense that
the VPN, as I said before,

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:55.910
was expensive, not just to
buy but also to maintain.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:58.940
Any change, any
increase meant that we

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.340
had some very, very hard
communications with Google

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:02.270
to change everything.

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:03.560
We were doing a lot
of the application,

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:05.930
we we're doing a lot of work
that Google would end up

00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:06.930
doing afterwards anyway.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.513
And thankfully,
again, they upstreamed

00:17:08.513 --> 00:17:10.640
a lot of what we did, so
it wasn't for nothing.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:13.369
But they definitely
do it better than us.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:15.380
So why should we do it?

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.990
And ultimately to ensure
that everything is correct,

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:20.560
we had to figure out all of
the undocumented endpoints

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:24.800
and all of the undocumented
IAM statements, which we still

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:27.829
recommend that you do,
but it's still a hardship.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:31.780
And now, back to Nick for a
brief philosophical interlude.

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:34.390
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS:
Thank you, Jaques.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:37.240
So the challenges that
we had were, in part,

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:39.310
driven, as Jaques
mentioned, by us

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:41.320
wanting to enable this
velocity and wanting

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:44.260
to enable people to harness
the power of the cloud

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:47.450
but also having very definite
security considerations

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:49.480
and security controls.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:53.077
We did go into this eyes wide
open that we were iterating.

00:17:53.077 --> 00:17:54.910
But as we were iterating,
we were racking up

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:56.560
a lot of tech debt.

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:58.990
And one of the
things that I believe

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:02.615
very philosophical that security
teams should do is to the quote

00:18:02.615 --> 00:18:04.990
that Rich Smith gave when he
was the director of security

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:05.890
at Etsy.

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:07.810
Security teams really
should be enablers

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:10.080
for the rest of engineering
and for the business.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:12.070
Any time security
becomes a blocker,

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:13.780
ultimately your
security posture will

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:16.954
suffer, because people will
find ways to route around you.

00:18:16.954 --> 00:18:18.370
And also, you'll
lose credibility,

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:20.720
because you're no longer
a business enabler.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.590
So the steps that we'd
taken, while there

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:26.680
were really great at getting us
into the cloud really quickly,

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:29.502
we fully understood that
we picked up this debt,

00:18:29.502 --> 00:18:31.210
and we were causing
this kind of friction

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.130
for the rest of
our business teams.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:36.340
And we really wanted a way
to resolve these issues

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:38.150
in a very cloud-native way.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:41.460
It seemed like we were doing
things the old-fashioned way.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:44.350
And this wasn't an ideal state
for us to iterate quickly

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:45.820
and to move faster.

00:18:45.820 --> 00:18:48.640
So we sat around, and
we thought about how

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:50.290
we could address these issues.

00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:53.110
And at this point, it
was really clear to us

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:55.150
that as a design
partner with Google,

00:18:55.150 --> 00:18:58.570
a lot of the value for
us would be in surfacing

00:18:58.570 --> 00:18:59.950
these issues to Google.

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:02.470
As a partner, we had to
surface our concerns,

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:04.210
and we had to talk to
them and understand

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:07.180
what was available for us
to address those concerns

00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:09.400
or what was potentially
roadmapped so that we could

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:12.460
make the right decisions
around investing and mitigating

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:14.320
some of our risks
in the short term

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:16.792
or actually just holding
off, but making sure

00:19:16.792 --> 00:19:19.000
that we weren't engineering
things that weren't going

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:23.324
to last and were just destroying
engineer productivity.

00:19:23.324 --> 00:19:25.240
One of the things that
was also of value added

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:28.690
from our perspective to Google
is, as we heard this morning,

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:31.390
to paraphrase one from
Diane about Google's mission

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:34.030
statement, Google
exists to organize

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:37.120
the world's information and
make it easily accessible.

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:39.594
In our case, we really
wanted to do the opposite.

00:19:39.594 --> 00:19:41.510
We wanted to organize
as much data as we could

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:44.330
but not make it accessible to
anyone outside of Two Sigma.

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:49.360
So this perspective, whilst
not unique to our industry,

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:52.924
was a little early for a lot of
the Google Cloud understanding,

00:19:52.924 --> 00:19:54.590
because most of the
[INAUDIBLE] adopters

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:56.802
are consumer-facing
organizations.

00:19:56.802 --> 00:19:58.510
The services are built
to enable somebody

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:01.690
to iterate really rapidly
and put things out there.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:03.340
So we thought this
two-way conversation

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:06.670
would be really helpful in
terms of, as partners, us

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:09.850
informing each other and then
developing native controls.

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:12.100
So I'm going to talk a little
more in-depth about some

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:13.683
of these controls
and some of the ones

00:20:13.683 --> 00:20:17.100
that are relevant to us and
the journey that we had.

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:18.910
And in this specific
use case, I just

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:21.460
want to point out that
obviously, security is

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:22.805
multiple layers in the stack.

00:20:22.805 --> 00:20:24.680
So you have to be
concerned about application

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:26.564
security, host security.

00:20:26.564 --> 00:20:28.730
Right now, I'm not going
to talk about any of those.

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:30.980
I'm just going to talk about
the cloud-native services

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:33.610
that we use to protect
the Google Cloud.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:35.334
Likewise, Jaques
touched on G Suite.

00:20:35.334 --> 00:20:37.000
I'm not going to talk
about that either,

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:40.547
because that could be
a whole talk by itself.

00:20:40.547 --> 00:20:42.380
So the first control
that was really helpful

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:46.190
for us was organization
policies and still is today.

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:49.130
Many of the risk factors that
Jaques had talked about--

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:53.630
bringing up VM instances
with external IP addresses,

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:55.940
the serial console
access, things

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.760
like being able to configure
which of our projects

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:01.070
was the source of
truth for images that

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:03.140
could be used in other
projects because we were

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:05.050
using our own custom images.

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:06.800
And then conversely,
the flip side of that

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:09.740
is making sure that the
custom configurations

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:11.660
that we were baking
into our own images,

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:13.650
we didn't want those to
just be usable anyway.

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:16.490
So also being able to restrict
where those were used by policy

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:17.900
was important to us.

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:19.670
And of course another
important policy

00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:22.020
is the fact that
we could configure

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:25.970
that only trusted user
principles from our domains

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:29.780
that we wanted to allow could be
added to any Google resources.

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:31.310
And organization
policies allowed

00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:34.670
us to do all of these things
very powerfully, very minimal

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:35.704
work involved.

00:21:35.704 --> 00:21:38.120
And we could configure them
at any level in the hierarchy,

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:41.120
from the organization
itself to specific folders

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:42.890
to actual individual
project levels.

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:46.290
And that gave us a lot of power.

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:48.230
As Alfred mentioned, I'm
talking a little more

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:50.630
about organization policies
and the specifics of what

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:54.050
we configured in a talk on
Thursday with the GCE team.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:56.010
So we can go into a
lot more depth there.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:58.490
But these are a really
powerful control,

00:21:58.490 --> 00:22:01.180
and we evolved a few of
these together with Google.

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:02.930
And one of the lovely
things that happened

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:04.940
was all of the manual
things that Google

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:06.590
had to do for us
on the back end,

00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:09.080
organization policies
eventually replaced those.

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:11.330
And they were completely
configurable and transparent

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:13.470
to us.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.340
VPC Service Controls
is another huge one.

00:22:16.340 --> 00:22:19.250
This one I really can't
understate the importance

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:21.530
of for our business.

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:23.600
In the context of this
talk, data exfiltration

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:26.000
is, again, our biggest
threat factor and our biggest

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:27.260
consideration.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:31.460
And VPC Service Controls really
allowed us to really extend

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:35.930
the use case of the Google Cloud
in a safe manner, which allowed

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:37.760
us to then protect our data.

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:42.030
So VPC Service Controls as a
concept is an interesting one.

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:44.930
If you think about an analogy of
traditional systems and network

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:47.280
security, you can really
control your perimeter

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:48.950
in a Google Cloud VPC.

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:51.680
You have complete control over
how you set up the routes.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:53.600
So as we do in many
of our projects,

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:56.450
you can just strip the
internet route entirely.

00:22:56.450 --> 00:22:59.480
You can also control firewall
policies, both ingress

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:02.360
and egress now that
that's available to us.

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:04.520
What do you do with API access?

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:07.370
If you want to configure and
work with managed services,

00:23:07.370 --> 00:23:10.250
which really is a lot of the
value of the Google Cloud--

00:23:10.250 --> 00:23:13.280
things like Google Cloud
Storage, BigQuery--

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:16.970
being able to work with those
APIs but also protect your data

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:19.130
and make sure that
your data is contained,

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:20.960
that's a really
significant problem.

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:25.370
And it led to us having
very complicated workflows.

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:27.860
Again, in the spirit of not
wanting to block innovation

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:29.990
from one of our
teams, what we did was

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:31.520
we ended up having
an environment

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:34.130
that we created that was
completely standalone and not

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:36.750
connected back to
our internal network.

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:38.917
We had a very convoluted
path with which

00:23:38.917 --> 00:23:41.000
the researchers and their
engineers and their team

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:42.950
were able to access
that environment.

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:45.260
And in there, they
had home servers

00:23:45.260 --> 00:23:48.300
that they could then use to
access services like BigQuery,

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:52.710
GCS, Dataproc, Dataflow,
and access the internet.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:55.410
The problem there was from
a security perspective,

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:58.040
we had to maintain two different
identities for these users.

00:23:58.040 --> 00:23:59.690
One was their actual
identity that they

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:01.640
could use from within
Two Sigma, where

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:04.104
we had to do a ton of
research and engineering

00:24:04.104 --> 00:24:06.020
and have proxies and all
sorts of other things

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:09.620
to ensure that that was a
read-only type role where

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:10.804
data couldn't be pushed out.

00:24:10.804 --> 00:24:13.220
And then, we had to maintain
a separate role for them that

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:15.790
was tied to their home servers
with which they could use

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:17.540
these services with abandon.

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:18.980
And that's not ideal.

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:20.840
You have researchers
and engineers

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:22.640
with duplicated environments.

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:24.950
They're not comfortable
on their own servers.

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:28.340
They also couldn't do things
like work on external data sets

00:24:28.340 --> 00:24:30.140
and then join those
with internal data sets

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:32.630
within Two Sigma and
really unlock the value

00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:34.640
of research in that way.

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:38.120
So what VPC Service Controls
allowed us to do is now

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:40.340
we can unlock those workflows.

00:24:40.340 --> 00:24:42.350
So at this point,
we have a situation

00:24:42.350 --> 00:24:44.630
where we have an
environment configured

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:46.910
where a researcher
within Two Sigma

00:24:46.910 --> 00:24:48.680
can work on their own
research environment

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:50.780
on their own servers,
their home servers.

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:52.580
And they can natively
access services

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:55.370
like Google Cloud
Storage and BigQuery.

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:57.740
And the power of that is
incredible in allowing

00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:00.070
our researchers to
really use internal data

00:25:00.070 --> 00:25:02.750
sets, external data,
and accelerate research

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:06.380
without producing massive
complicated infrastructure.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:09.802
Again, there's a lot of nuance
to VPC Service Controls.

00:25:09.802 --> 00:25:12.260
There's another talk that I'm
speaking in with that product

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:14.270
team on Thursday as well.

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.100
We will talk about
more of the mechanics

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.030
of the technical depth of it.

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:20.900
But this was a really key thing
where we worked very tightly

00:25:20.900 --> 00:25:21.410
with Google.

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:24.300
The collaboration was huge.

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:27.290
It was conceived here a year
ago in some meetings Jaques

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:28.760
had over lunch with
Sam [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:30.850
and some of the
product team, and we've

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:32.870
collaborated really
closely over the last year

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:34.490
and seen the product evolve.

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:35.670
We used it extensively.

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:37.160
We pen tested it.

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:41.270
And we did a lot of research
on it as early-access adopters.

00:25:41.270 --> 00:25:43.220
And really, we even
contributed to some

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:46.610
of the evolution, things like
bridging of zones, and some

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:48.410
of the more
complicated scenarios,

00:25:48.410 --> 00:25:50.570
and representing our
own internal network

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:54.140
as a zone for VPC service
control, so a lot of depth

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:54.750
there.

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:56.500
But this is one of the
massive things that

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.720
unlocked a lot of velocity for
our business in a very, very

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:02.170
safe manner.

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:05.680
Jaques touched on our woes
with VPN connectivity.

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:08.110
Dedicated Interconnect
now really gives us

00:26:08.110 --> 00:26:10.750
private dedicated connectivity
to the Google Cloud.

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:12.970
The bandwidth we have is huge.

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:16.780
And we no longer need to
tax our internet proxies.

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:19.600
We no longer need to maintain
really complicated policies

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:21.400
for a lot of use cases
on those proxies,

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:23.560
because the
connectivity is private.

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:26.544
And this coupled together
with the VPC service controls

00:26:26.544 --> 00:26:28.960
and some of the other things
I talked about earlier really

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:31.990
has been instrumental
in creating

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:33.820
that environment
that's an extension

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:35.560
of our internal
network and the tools

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.440
that researchers
are comfortable with

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:39.580
and enabling us to
really accelerate

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.620
the use of the cloud
across our business.

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:45.310
Once we had that initial
use case proven out,

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:47.770
a lot of other business
units within the firm

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:49.360
are really now
interested in how they

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:52.660
can adopt these services using
this private connectivity.

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:55.870
So that was a big thing for us.

00:26:55.870 --> 00:26:59.170
Finally, IAM
conditions-- again, this

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:04.060
is a very important element in
our overall security posture.

00:27:04.060 --> 00:27:06.490
We believe very strongly in
defense in depth and layered

00:27:06.490 --> 00:27:07.990
defenses.

00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:09.760
And IAM conditions
really allow us

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:12.700
to complement the very
coarse-grained access control

00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:14.560
that something like
VPC Service Controls

00:27:14.560 --> 00:27:16.960
gives us with a very,
very fine-grained

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:19.690
and super granular
set of conditions

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:21.260
where we can meld
those together.

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:23.890
So right now, I can't
talk in too much depth

00:27:23.890 --> 00:27:26.810
about IAM conditions, because
there's a talk on it tomorrow.

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:29.290
And some of the features
are going to beta tomorrow.

00:27:29.290 --> 00:27:31.400
But if you do want
some depth, again,

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:33.250
please reach out to
the product team.

00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:35.960
Chris Law, the product
manager, is fantastic.

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:38.290
What I can say is
that IAM conditions

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:40.630
allows us to now take
some of the principles

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:44.200
that VPC Service Controls give
us for things like controlling

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:46.930
where we want to allow
access to APIs from

00:27:46.930 --> 00:27:50.380
and actually embed those
into IAM in some cases where

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:53.890
VPC Service Controls may
not be the right solution

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:56.830
or in cases where we want it
as an additional layer of depth

00:27:56.830 --> 00:27:58.450
in our defense and depth stack.

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.250
So really powerful,
again, and really

00:28:00.250 --> 00:28:03.400
helpful in terms of Google
listening to our concerns

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:06.040
and really building
solutions that enable these,

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:07.660
and allowing us to
adopt these early

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:09.572
and test them and help
with the iteration.

00:28:09.572 --> 00:28:10.905
Those things are super valuable.

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:17.110
So again, over the
years, it's almost

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:18.580
been hammered into
security people

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:21.120
that no matter how much
you focus on prevention

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:24.020
and trying to keep bad
things from happening.

00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:26.470
There's an adage that if you
think you're not compromised,

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:28.303
you've probably been
compromised for a while

00:28:28.303 --> 00:28:30.120
and just don't realize it.

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:32.500
In terms of complementing
that security posture,

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:34.840
it's important to also
have detection and response

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:36.184
controls.

00:28:36.184 --> 00:28:37.600
And Cloud Security
Command Center,

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:41.050
again, is a very cloud-native
product from Google

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:43.870
that allows us to have a single
console in a single interface

00:28:43.870 --> 00:28:46.960
with which we can monitor the
security posture of our Google

00:28:46.960 --> 00:28:47.980
Cloud.

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:50.020
Again, we were very
early adopters.

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:54.340
We worked very tightly with
Andy and his team about CSCC.

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:56.955
We helped it evolve.

00:28:56.955 --> 00:28:59.080
One of my colleagues is
speaking on how we actually

00:28:59.080 --> 00:29:01.810
use CSCC in terms of
monitoring our security

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:03.280
posture for the Cloud.

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:06.130
But again, a lot of
the security teams

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:08.560
that start to move
towards the cloud,

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:10.360
you see the kind
of mindset of, we

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:12.070
want to replicate
the security stack

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:15.087
and the security controls we
have on premise in the cloud.

00:29:15.087 --> 00:29:17.170
And that's really the wrong
way to think about it.

00:29:17.170 --> 00:29:19.130
The problems are
fundamentally different.

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:22.519
If you think about-- and anybody
here who's a user of the cloud,

00:29:22.519 --> 00:29:25.060
you know that the scale of the
cloud and the pace of adoption

00:29:25.060 --> 00:29:27.700
is huge compared to what
you have on premise.

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:30.460
And again, you'd be
creating tech debt.

00:29:30.460 --> 00:29:32.410
You'd be creating
suboptimal solutions

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:34.420
by trying to bring
everything back in house

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:36.232
and put it in the same console.

00:29:36.232 --> 00:29:37.690
And sure, there is
some value there

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:39.310
in having a single
pane of glass.

00:29:39.310 --> 00:29:41.800
But really if you're
thinking cloud-natively,

00:29:41.800 --> 00:29:44.200
you want to use
cloud-based solutions

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:45.735
to deal with the cloud.

00:29:45.735 --> 00:29:47.110
And Cloud Security
Command Center

00:29:47.110 --> 00:29:49.780
is a very strong
evolving technology

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:54.020
in that space, which we're
really excited about.

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:56.900
As a cousin to CSCC is
the open-source project,

00:29:56.900 --> 00:29:59.750
Forseti, that Google sponsors.

00:29:59.750 --> 00:30:02.390
Forseti's really well
used and contributed to

00:30:02.390 --> 00:30:04.130
by companies like Spotify.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:07.380
We contribute to it from
an open-source perspective.

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:09.050
It really is a
powerful tool for us,

00:30:09.050 --> 00:30:12.440
because historically,
we had a model where

00:30:12.440 --> 00:30:14.780
when we were making changes
in the Google Cloud,

00:30:14.780 --> 00:30:17.900
we'd go through a very strong
upfront review process.

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:18.889
We'd review the code.

00:30:18.889 --> 00:30:20.180
We'd review the implementation.

00:30:20.180 --> 00:30:22.070
We'd go to deployment.

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:24.230
And then, things
would be deployed.

00:30:24.230 --> 00:30:26.090
And who audits that
infrastructure?

00:30:26.090 --> 00:30:30.170
Sure, we could audit it, but
we have multiple projects.

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:32.580
Auditing it manually
is less than ideal.

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:34.610
So this really
didn't scale for us.

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:36.680
What we wanted is--
again, thinking

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:39.590
in a cloud-native approach,
we wanted a perspective

00:30:39.590 --> 00:30:42.620
where we could automate
our security posture.

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:44.060
We wanted to have
a place where we

00:30:44.060 --> 00:30:49.560
could aggregate our inventory
at any given point in time

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:51.680
and then apply
security policy to that

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:54.080
and validate that our
assumptions are still upheld,

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:55.760
just in an automated fashion.

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:57.020
So we deployed for Forseti.

00:30:57.020 --> 00:30:59.502
Forseti was really helpful
for us from that perspective.

00:30:59.502 --> 00:31:01.460
And actually, a lot of
the work that we've done

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:05.150
is integrating Forseti so that
when Forseti fires an alert,

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:08.650
it will actually be viewable in
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:11.506
And again, we're using the
cloud to secure the cloud.

00:31:11.506 --> 00:31:13.880
And you'll notice I didn't
talk at all about Stackdriver,

00:31:13.880 --> 00:31:15.950
about logs and monitoring.

00:31:15.950 --> 00:31:17.660
I think those are just a given.

00:31:17.660 --> 00:31:19.340
We're just talking
about the next level

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:24.650
of monitoring in the cloud,
not the fundamentals.

00:31:24.650 --> 00:31:26.540
So we've talked about
a lot of technologies

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:30.750
and a lot of tools that helped
us get to a decent security

00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:31.250
posture.

00:31:31.250 --> 00:31:33.980
But it's also important
to talk about process.

00:31:33.980 --> 00:31:36.860
And I have a story that
Jaques will really--

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:39.450
it will resonate with Jaques
and with one of my colleagues,

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:41.750
Chris, in the audience.

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:44.960
The way that we did deployments
in the cloud, historically,

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:47.130
also evolved over time.

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:48.830
Back in the early
days, we had a couple

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:51.050
of people who had
org admin privileges.

00:31:51.050 --> 00:31:52.790
And those were the
people who you went to

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:54.560
if you needed changes made.

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:57.040
And that was OK, and it helped
us iterate really quickly.

00:31:57.040 --> 00:31:59.630
But again, thinking about
our fundamental security

00:31:59.630 --> 00:32:02.120
principles, that
put us in a position

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:05.372
where if that person were
compromised by an attacker,

00:32:05.372 --> 00:32:07.080
the keys to the kingdom
were right there,

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:09.360
and they could do
whatever they wanted.

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:10.890
So that was less than ideal.

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:12.800
So we took a step
back, and we thought

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:15.650
about how we could try
to mitigate that threat.

00:32:15.650 --> 00:32:17.020
And we came up with v2.

00:32:17.020 --> 00:32:20.520
And v2 was that nobody had
org admin privileges anymore.

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:24.440
We created one user principal
who was our super org admin,

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:26.630
and that user was used
for break glass changes.

00:32:26.630 --> 00:32:28.690
And in terms of our
security posture,

00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:33.620
changes that required anything
to do with storage or identity

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:36.620
and access management or
networking configurations,

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:39.080
anything that could open
up systems with data,

00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:41.420
we considered those to
be break glass changes.

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:43.610
So what we did was
we'd have a workflow

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:46.430
where when somebody needed
one of these changes,

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:48.680
they'd submit the
change request to us.

00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:51.309
The security team would review
the change and approve it.

00:32:51.309 --> 00:32:52.850
And then what we'd
have to do is we'd

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:55.349
have to pair up with someone
in the public cloud engineering

00:32:55.349 --> 00:32:58.100
team, because they had the
password to the super user

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:01.160
account, and we had the
corresponding security key.

00:33:01.160 --> 00:33:04.200
And then, you had
separation of duties,

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:05.450
and you could make the change.

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:08.730
And that was fantastic for us
from a security perspective,

00:33:08.730 --> 00:33:10.790
but from an agility
perspective, it wasn't great.

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:12.860
There were so many manual steps.

00:33:12.860 --> 00:33:16.310
Ultimately, it culminated in
you having two resources, who

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:17.676
are all really busy.

00:33:17.676 --> 00:33:19.550
And somebody was just
sitting around watching

00:33:19.550 --> 00:33:22.250
somebody else type things
or click around, which

00:33:22.250 --> 00:33:23.722
was really suboptimal.

00:33:23.722 --> 00:33:25.430
So again, we took a
step back and thought

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:27.560
about how we could use
cloud-native technologies

00:33:27.560 --> 00:33:31.070
in our own processes to
enable these workflows.

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:33.470
And what we netted out at
was doing a project together

00:33:33.470 --> 00:33:36.680
with Jaques, again, where we
built a solution for automated

00:33:36.680 --> 00:33:38.090
deployment.

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:40.970
We built this in a
manner that now when

00:33:40.970 --> 00:33:42.470
somebody wants one
of those changes,

00:33:42.470 --> 00:33:44.840
they'll merge Google Cloud
Deployment Manager template

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:48.077
code to a source control repo.

00:33:48.077 --> 00:33:49.910
We'll review it and
approve it and merge it.

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:53.090
And that kicks off an
event-driven CI/CD pipeline

00:33:53.090 --> 00:33:56.352
that will then have an
artifact ready to be deployed.

00:33:56.352 --> 00:33:58.310
On the other side of
that, because the security

00:33:58.310 --> 00:34:00.500
team doesn't know when
the optimal deployment

00:34:00.500 --> 00:34:03.650
time is for an engineering
team, it doesn't automatically

00:34:03.650 --> 00:34:04.190
deploy.

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:05.750
It's just ready to deploy.

00:34:05.750 --> 00:34:08.420
And we have a UI that's
protected by Identity-Aware

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:10.969
Proxy that then allows
the user to log in

00:34:10.969 --> 00:34:13.159
and then hit a button to
deploy the deployment.

00:34:13.159 --> 00:34:14.060
They can roll back.

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:15.659
They can do all sorts of things.

00:34:15.659 --> 00:34:18.469
Again, Google enabled us to do
this with their technologies

00:34:18.469 --> 00:34:22.846
like IAP, like Cloud Functions,
like App Engine Flex--

00:34:22.846 --> 00:34:24.679
really, really powerful
tooling and thinking

00:34:24.679 --> 00:34:27.480
about things in a
Cloud-native way.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:30.534
Finally, I won't harp on this
too much, but least privilege

00:34:30.534 --> 00:34:33.810
is really critical to
us as a core tenet.

00:34:33.810 --> 00:34:36.150
And again, Jaques had spoken
about where we started out

00:34:36.150 --> 00:34:39.120
with just the owner,
editor, viewer.

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:42.510
Today, we don't use anything
but custom IAM roles.

00:34:42.510 --> 00:34:46.612
So anytime somebody needs
to perform a workflow

00:34:46.612 --> 00:34:48.820
or we have a new team that's
onboarding to the cloud,

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:53.310
we spend time to understand
what each specific role

00:34:53.310 --> 00:34:55.510
within that business
needs to do their job,

00:34:55.510 --> 00:34:58.650
and we craft an IAM role with
exactly the permissions that

00:34:58.650 --> 00:35:01.440
are required for that
and give it to that team.

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:03.720
That allows us to
also share those roles

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:05.220
across multiple teams.

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:09.300
Again, I talked about
templates and source control.

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:10.770
That's really powerful for us.

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:13.440
And in the event that that
user were compromised,

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:17.000
we absolutely know what the
impact of that compromise

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:18.840
is based on what
their privileges are.

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:22.020
So again, customized IAM roles
went to beta a while back--

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:24.390
a really powerful
tool for us, even over

00:35:24.390 --> 00:35:26.940
and above the curated roles
that Google now publishes.

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:33.320
So we talked a lot about very
specific controls, things

00:35:33.320 --> 00:35:35.270
that we had to
address our problems.

00:35:35.270 --> 00:35:37.180
Obviously, we only
had 50 minutes,

00:35:37.180 --> 00:35:39.500
so there were a lot that
we couldn't get into.

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:41.960
But some of the quick
takeaways in terms of lessons

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:45.380
learned from our journey
and our process--

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:47.840
as if I haven't said
it enough in this talk,

00:35:47.840 --> 00:35:50.900
it's really important to
collaborate with Google.

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:55.460
We work very tightly with our
account team, our business

00:35:55.460 --> 00:35:57.680
manager, our [INAUDIBLE],,
our customer engineer

00:35:57.680 --> 00:35:59.012
are fantastic.

00:35:59.012 --> 00:36:01.220
But we also work really
closely with product managers

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:02.120
and with engineers.

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:05.630
And again, I can't tell
you how empowering it

00:36:05.630 --> 00:36:09.450
is to be working on a product
and actually be on the phone

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:11.377
or in a Hangout with
the engineers that

00:36:11.377 --> 00:36:12.460
are building that product.

00:36:12.460 --> 00:36:15.470
That sort of level
of iteration really

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:20.590
is what enables quick velocity,
acceleration in the cloud.

00:36:20.590 --> 00:36:23.180
That partnership is fundamental.

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:26.750
And again, Google also needs to
understand what your problems

00:36:26.750 --> 00:36:28.370
are in order to resolve them.

00:36:28.370 --> 00:36:30.867
And we've actually seen value
not only for our own firm

00:36:30.867 --> 00:36:33.200
in some of the controls that
I talked about that evolved

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:35.420
through some of our issues,
but also, everybody else

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:36.440
gets the benefit from them.

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:38.523
So in the same way as it's
important to contribute

00:36:38.523 --> 00:36:40.790
to open source, it's
important to work with vendors

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:43.081
and help them understand what
the problems are in order

00:36:43.081 --> 00:36:45.980
to resolve those.

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:48.080
Sandboxing is interesting.

00:36:48.080 --> 00:36:50.360
Again, thinking
about velocity, it's

00:36:50.360 --> 00:36:53.000
really easy in an
organization like ours

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:55.160
with a security posture
that's really tight

00:36:55.160 --> 00:36:58.365
to just stifle workflows by not
allowing people to do things.

00:36:58.365 --> 00:37:00.240
But again, that's not
very cloud-native-like.

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:02.660
How do you enable people
to iterate and experiment

00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:05.420
with the freedom that they need
but also maintain your security

00:37:05.420 --> 00:37:06.800
posture?

00:37:06.800 --> 00:37:09.650
The answer is difficult. So
the approach that we took

00:37:09.650 --> 00:37:11.930
is segmenting out
development entirely.

00:37:11.930 --> 00:37:16.250
So we have a separate G Suite
domain and a separate Google

00:37:16.250 --> 00:37:17.840
Cloud Platform
organization linked

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:20.150
to that that's just not
accessible from within Two

00:37:20.150 --> 00:37:20.952
Sigma.

00:37:20.952 --> 00:37:22.410
You have to be on
our guest network

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:25.209
or on an unmanaged device
to access that environment.

00:37:25.209 --> 00:37:26.750
And what that gives
us is the ability

00:37:26.750 --> 00:37:29.390
to allow people the freedoms to
have the privileges that they

00:37:29.390 --> 00:37:32.810
need to iterate and develop
and also protect our IP,

00:37:32.810 --> 00:37:35.090
because we can't get
to our IP from there.

00:37:35.090 --> 00:37:37.880
So that ultimately led to
even better workflows for us.

00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:40.580
Because then when we have
a new team or a team that's

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:43.370
considering a new project,
they already really understand

00:37:43.370 --> 00:37:46.524
what it is they need from
the Cloud Platform and IAM

00:37:46.524 --> 00:37:47.690
and could just tell us that.

00:37:47.690 --> 00:37:50.960
And we could easily review that,
approve it, and roll it out.

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:55.430
I talked a lot about
being cloud-native.

00:37:55.430 --> 00:37:58.680
Again, there are obvious
security trade-offs here.

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:01.050
If you want the
ultimate set of control,

00:38:01.050 --> 00:38:04.020
you'd build everything on top
of Google Cloud Engine VMs,

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:06.640
and then you maintain pretty
much as much as you want.

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:07.890
But there's a trade-off there.

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:09.850
Then, you have to worry
about administration.

00:38:09.850 --> 00:38:11.470
You have to worry
about patching.

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:14.084
You have to worry about
vulnerability management.

00:38:14.084 --> 00:38:16.500
The trade-off there is to use
completely managed services,

00:38:16.500 --> 00:38:19.530
something like Dataproc,
where you don't have to worry

00:38:19.530 --> 00:38:21.340
about any of those things.

00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:25.970
But in turn, you lose that level
of transparency in the stack.

00:38:25.970 --> 00:38:28.320
And it's difficult. There's
no right or wrong answer.

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:30.960
Every use case is
business-risk-dependent,

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:31.830
data-dependent.

00:38:31.830 --> 00:38:33.660
But the key thing
as a security team

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:35.577
is to understand what
those trade-offs are

00:38:35.577 --> 00:38:38.160
and present them to the business
so that the business can make

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:41.700
the appropriate decision based
on the value of the project

00:38:41.700 --> 00:38:43.470
and the value of the data.

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:45.300
So again, cloud-native
controls--

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:48.300
really our posture is
wherever we can use something

00:38:48.300 --> 00:38:51.172
that Google's built, they'll
support it, develop it,

00:38:51.172 --> 00:38:53.005
and maintain it better
than any custom hacks

00:38:53.005 --> 00:38:54.430
that we'd have to put in.

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:57.760
So that's our preference.

00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:00.910
Layered defenses-- again, I
spoke about defense in depth

00:39:00.910 --> 00:39:02.807
extensively, so I won't
spend too much time.

00:39:02.807 --> 00:39:04.390
But you don't want
to be in a position

00:39:04.390 --> 00:39:07.450
where a single
compromise or a mistake

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:09.610
or even a failure on
the cloud provider side

00:39:09.610 --> 00:39:12.100
would lead to you
having a really bad day.

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:14.295
If you're one mistake
or one compromise

00:39:14.295 --> 00:39:16.420
away from being on the
front page of the newspaper,

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:17.810
that's a bad place to be.

00:39:17.810 --> 00:39:20.110
So the more that you
can layer defenses,

00:39:20.110 --> 00:39:24.190
even if it takes more work,
even if it's unnecessary,

00:39:24.190 --> 00:39:25.060
I like insurance.

00:39:25.060 --> 00:39:26.320
We have an insurance business.

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:29.830
So I am in that space.

00:39:29.830 --> 00:39:33.210
And the last takeaway from us
is it's important to move fast.

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:35.020
Again, a lot of
organizations that

00:39:35.020 --> 00:39:37.450
are very security-sensitive
have a tendency

00:39:37.450 --> 00:39:42.490
to take a lot of time in review
and, rightly so, the analysis

00:39:42.490 --> 00:39:44.070
and getting in depth.

00:39:44.070 --> 00:39:47.470
But again, you're blocking
the business's velocity.

00:39:47.470 --> 00:39:49.680
And people are going to
find ways to work around you

00:39:49.680 --> 00:39:51.910
if you take six months to
review one new service.

00:39:51.910 --> 00:39:53.560
So find workflows.

00:39:53.560 --> 00:39:56.910
And again, to borrow a quote
from somebody else, Jason Chan,

00:39:56.910 --> 00:39:59.026
who is an engineering
director at Netflix,

00:39:59.026 --> 00:40:00.400
he said, securities
job should be

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:02.800
to build guardrails, not gates.

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:05.020
So ultimately,
we're not there yet,

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:07.270
but my team's goal
is to enable--

00:40:07.270 --> 00:40:09.890
none of our developers have to
think about security at all.

00:40:09.890 --> 00:40:11.297
We could just build a platform.

00:40:11.297 --> 00:40:12.880
We can work with our
partners, Google,

00:40:12.880 --> 00:40:14.754
to build a platform
where developers can just

00:40:14.754 --> 00:40:18.430
do their jobs without ever
having to think about security.

00:40:18.430 --> 00:40:21.170
So on that, I'd like to
hand back over to Alfred

00:40:21.170 --> 00:40:25.600
to wrap up with some final
thoughts and final notes.

00:40:28.610 --> 00:40:30.470
ALFRED SPECTOR:
So I hope you got

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:32.460
from that sort of a
couple of thoughts.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:35.690
One is we were pretty
early adopter in this.

00:40:35.690 --> 00:40:40.670
And you heard about what we
did in that stage from Jaques

00:40:40.670 --> 00:40:42.530
in his presentation.

00:40:42.530 --> 00:40:45.560
When I got to Two Sigma
just about three years ago,

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:49.040
we effectively didn't use the
cloud for much of anything--

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:51.840
I don't know, maybe a
couple of tiny things.

00:40:51.840 --> 00:40:56.810
And we were fairly able, because
of Jaques's and few others'

00:40:56.810 --> 00:40:59.390
work, to get to
a situation where

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:02.930
maybe half of our compute
load was sort of on the cloud.

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:06.290
Although, we did it in ways
that were not as cloud-native

00:41:06.290 --> 00:41:08.030
as we would like,
and we were not

00:41:08.030 --> 00:41:09.890
able to fully use
all the services

00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:12.380
that we knew that were coming.

00:41:12.380 --> 00:41:14.240
As we got to the
second phase of this,

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:15.950
working more
closely with Google,

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:20.210
we were able to use standard
technologies after certainly

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:22.495
providing input as to
what they should be,

00:41:22.495 --> 00:41:23.720
but able to use those.

00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:25.730
And now, we've
gotten to that stage

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:28.640
where not only are we doing a
lot of compute in the cloud,

00:41:28.640 --> 00:41:31.100
we're doing a lot more
heterogeneous computing

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:34.340
in the cloud using a lot
more different services.

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:37.040
And we do think that's
the future of all of this,

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:39.770
is that I don't think any
of us, unless maybe we're

00:41:39.770 --> 00:41:42.170
Google or a very,
very large company,

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:45.890
can build all the things
that we will want to use.

00:41:45.890 --> 00:41:46.970
They're just too big.

00:41:46.970 --> 00:41:50.020
We can't build BigQuery
or something like that.

00:41:50.020 --> 00:41:52.320
It's a huge project to go do.

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:56.390
So we're very excited
about the future.

00:41:56.390 --> 00:42:00.020
We think that Google
now understands

00:42:00.020 --> 00:42:03.180
a lot of these principles of--

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:05.570
that really come
from the notion that

00:42:05.570 --> 00:42:07.970
ensuring that data
is not universally

00:42:07.970 --> 00:42:10.520
accessible and useful, that's
not the only thing right.

00:42:10.520 --> 00:42:12.980
You need to be able to
control that access.

00:42:12.980 --> 00:42:14.960
And you need to be
able to control it

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:18.710
in a way, as Nick said very
eloquently, that you're not

00:42:18.710 --> 00:42:22.580
one mistake or one
attack away from being

00:42:22.580 --> 00:42:24.800
on the front page of
the "New York Times,"

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:26.150
as it has been said.

00:42:26.150 --> 00:42:27.740
So we certainly don't--

00:42:27.740 --> 00:42:30.020
we think that seems
to now be the case.

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:33.530
And we're very excited about
being able to rapidly move

00:42:33.530 --> 00:42:36.440
forward on a lot more fronts.

00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:39.350
These are the talks
that we're giving.

00:42:39.350 --> 00:42:40.700
So on the security--

00:42:40.700 --> 00:42:43.280
I guess it's already
kind of late on Tuesday.

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:45.140
So without a time
machine, you can't go back

00:42:45.140 --> 00:42:46.610
to the earlier talks.

00:42:46.610 --> 00:42:47.830
That, we do not have.

00:42:47.830 --> 00:42:51.560
If we did, that would be quite
interesting in our business.

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:55.430
And on Wednesday
and Thursday, you'll

00:42:55.430 --> 00:42:58.850
see the talks, though,
that are listed in security

00:42:58.850 --> 00:43:04.090
and, as I mentioned, the
serverless computing talk.

00:43:04.090 --> 00:43:07.010
The Halite talk, I think is
at the same time slot, right?

00:43:07.010 --> 00:43:09.410
It's also at 4:35
in the afternoon

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:10.910
if you want to have
some fun and see

00:43:10.910 --> 00:43:14.960
how maybe AI will continue
to be explored in ways

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:16.840
that are really challenging.

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:19.340
So we should open this
up to some questions

00:43:19.340 --> 00:43:22.430
after the obligatory,
we're hiring.

00:43:22.430 --> 00:43:25.160
We do exciting things,
as you can tell.

00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:27.770
We're trying always to
be on the forefront.

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:29.580
More than any other
firm that we know of,

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:32.180
we get to answer the question
with all of the security

00:43:32.180 --> 00:43:35.180
and computation, what is the
effect of machine learning

00:43:35.180 --> 00:43:39.230
on AI in the entire world
of economic optimization?

00:43:39.230 --> 00:43:41.990
No one knows the answer to
that question, in my opinion.

00:43:41.990 --> 00:43:44.390
I've been in this field
leading research teams

00:43:44.390 --> 00:43:46.250
for many decades.

00:43:46.250 --> 00:43:48.530
We know small areas
where it's useful.

00:43:48.530 --> 00:43:50.520
But we don't know the breadth.

00:43:50.520 --> 00:43:54.380
So the firm is dedicated to
some really interesting problems

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:57.110
that, if solved, will make
economies in the world,

00:43:57.110 --> 00:44:01.059
I think, far more efficient and
make our firm very successful.

00:44:01.059 --> 00:44:02.600
So thank you very
much for your time.

00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:04.433
Let's take some questions
if there are some.

00:44:04.433 --> 00:44:08.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

